WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.862
DNSSEC lets a resolver verify
that an answer is authentic.

2
00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:05.859
It doesn't encrypt anything;

3
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:10.462
NIST is explicit that DNSSEC and
encrypted DNS are complements.

4
00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:15.700
Federal policy has required DNSSEC
for agency domains since 2009.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.569
On internal zones, NIST's new guidance is
more cautious than the whitepaper was:

6
00:00:20.569 --> 00:00:25.663
signing them is likely of limited value,
and recommended against unless required.

7
00:00:25.663 --> 00:00:30.766
So the program signs external zones
always, validates on every agency resolver,

8
00:00:30.766 --> 00:00:35.638
and signs internal zones only where
the system's authorization requires it.

9
00:00:35.638 --> 00:00:37.263
The platforms aren't equal.

10
00:00:37.263 --> 00:00:41.358
Windows Server's default zone-signing
key is below NIST's floor,

11
00:00:41.358 --> 00:00:43.563
so the program uses stronger keys.

12
00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:47.116
And Azure DNS and Route 53
sign public zones only,

13
00:00:47.116 --> 00:00:51.000
so neither gives the agency
validation it controls.
